The normalizer N_G(P), derived explicitly
----------------------------------------

The semisimple order19 natural isometry has one dual pair of nontrivial
cubic constituents and one trivial constituent: ord_19(11)=3 and no power
of11 in this order3 subgroup is -1. The nondegenerate form pairs a cubic
with its distinct dual. Thus its fixed space is one nonisotropic line,
and it is its unique rational invariant line. This is the unchanged proof
in [G]. Every normalizer of P preserves that same line. Its full Cayley
line stabilizer is split SL3(11):2: the unitary stabilizer has order
2*11^3*(11^3+1)*(11^2-1), not divisible by19.

Let F=SL3(11) be the point kernel of that line stabilizer. P is in F and
acts irreducibly on the natural three-space. Identify that space with
E=F_(11^3). Its commuting endomorphism field is E, since a generator of P
has irreducible cubic minimal polynomial. Consequently its GL3 centralizer
is E^*, and its SL3 centralizer is the norm-one cyclic torus

  T=ker(Norm_E/F11),             |T|=(11^3-1)/(11-1)=133=7*19.

Any linear normalizer of P normalizes F11[P]=E. Its induced F11-algebra
automorphism is a power of Frobenius, and its remaining factor centralizes
E, hence lies in E^*. Conversely multiplication maps and Frobenius normalize
P. The Frobenius map f:x ->x^11 has order3 and determinant1: its determinant
lies in F11^* and has order dividing both3 and10. Therefore

  N_F(P)=T:<f>,                 |N_F(P)|=133*3.

For completeness the graph half splits in a way which commutes with f.
Use the nondegenerate symmetric trace pairing (x,y)=Tr_E/F11(xy).
The automorphism tau:A ->(A^*)^-1, adjoint-inverse for that pairing, is a
graph involution; it sends multiplication by alpha to multiplication by
alpha^-1. Frobenius is an isometry of the trace pairing, so tau(f)=f.
This graph automorphism is the one in the full natural line normalizer,
up to an inner change of coordinates: any GL3 conjugating factor can be
rescaled to determinant1 because cubing is a bijection on F11^*.

It follows that, as a literal subgroup over this fixed P,

  N=N_G(P)=T:(<f> x <tau>) = C133:C6.

The action on T is generated by the exponent11 of f and inversion by tau.
No larger normalizer is hidden outside the line stabilizer, by uniqueness
of the P-line. The normalizer order therefore is798. This is also consistent
with Wilson Section5.9.1's explicit (7x19):6 normalizer in the J1 construction;
the argument here proves the needed P-normalizer instead of identifying two
normalizers merely by equal order.

Any involution b normalizing P and inverting p has image tau in N/T.
Indeed the six induced powers on P are +/-11^i, and only -11^0 is inversion.
It therefore inverts all of T. Since T is odd, its square map is bijective;
conjugating the displayed complement by a suitable torus element carries
tau to this b. Hence

  K=C_N(b) is cyclic of order6.

Its order3 subgroup is canonical for the actual P and actual b.

