Why every end contains B
-----------------------

Let H be either L or R. It contains P and b, since p=bc. It also contains
a V4: <a,c> for L, <b,d> for R. If H=G, it contains B immediately. Suppose
H is proper, and place it in a maximal subgroup using classification input1.

If H<=J with J isomorphic to J1, input3 shows H=J: otherwise a proper
maximal of J containing H would be19:6, which cannot contain its V4.
Then N_H(P) has order114 and contains P,b, and lies in N_G(P). The uniqueness
just proved forces N_H(P)=B.

If H<=M with M isomorphic to SL3(11):2, let F=SL3(11) and H0=H intersection F.
P<=H0 because its order is odd. The V4 maps to M/F=C2 with a nontrivial
kernel, so H0 contains an involution. Input2 rules out H0<F: the only
proper overgroups of P in F have odd order. Thus H0=F.
Also b is outside F, since b normalizes P and the exact N_F(P) above is odd.
Therefore H=M. Every such M containing P is the full stabilizer of the
unique P-line; it contains N_G(P), and in particular B.

In all alternatives B<=H. Applying this to both ends gives

  B <= L intersection R,       |B|=114>38=|D|,

which proves the statement. The proof uses no end order engine, orbit
closure, moved-line witness, order4 witness, half-turn rescaling argument,
or exact full-group closure. Full generation and the remaining original
predicates are not needed after a proved failure of this necessary IP clause.

